{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795367541,"nonce":"wKd6wpWvOQaIpaHOFNyJfacKuJw=","message":"Step 4 is done in 2c6bcbcb, behind ZYNTAX_IMMIX_THREADS=1, which is still off by default. A pointer that a thread plugin queues, returns or passes to a new thread now stays alive until the receiving side has it. The commit 0cc07e57 before it only formats the thread plugin. The plugin is a path dependency of the compiler's tests now, so cargo fmt --all reaches it.\n\nAudit. Where a heap pointer sat in memory that no collection reads:\n\nFixed.\n- Start-up argument of thread_spawn and the three closure spawns: it sat in a Rust closure on the heap. The spawner holds it. The new thread attaches and then lets go.\n- A new thread's own stack: a thread joined the world only at its first allocation. A thread that read what it was given held pointers that nobody scanned. A started thread now attaches first thing.\n- Thread result: it sat in the join handle's packet. The thread holds it before it exits, and thread_join lets go.\n- Channel payloads: they sat in the queue. Send holds them, and every receive variant lets go.\n- Channel close: closing the receiver drains the queue and lets go. A failed send lets go. The bridge thread of a bounded channel does the same for what it forwards.\n- Band pool workers attach before their first job. They already registered at their first wait, so this is now explicit.\n- Promise then and catch threads attach before they run the callback.\n\nChecked, no change needed.\n- Closures from compiled code: zyntax_closure_to_zrtl copies the environment into held storage, so a collection already reads it.\n- Band pool jobs carry the dispatcher's stack slot. The dispatcher's own roots cover it while it waits.\n- Promise state machines were held already. Promise values are host values (ZyntaxValue), not program pointers.\n- The http and net plugin threads carry Rust strings and integer handles only. Tier-up and compile threads touch no program object.\n\nHow. The SDK gains zrtl::heap::attach, hold, unhold and a Hold guard, installed by one set_roots call. The embed runtime installs them next to the allocator. The plugin loader reaches them through a new _zrtl_set_roots export. The host side is immix_heap::attach and three C functions in pool_alloc that wrap host_heap::hold.\n\nTests.\n- The thread plugin has five tests with a fake host that records every hold. If the hold it checks is removed, each test fails. The checks cover the result, the send, the close and the order of the start-up hold against attach.\n- The new integration test thread_plugin_collect runs the real plugin against the real Immix heap at a 256 KB floor. Sixteen threads per round get a list through their argument and return another through their result, while the main thread allocates. A producer queues 200 lists that only the queue refers to. A closed channel releases what it queued. Every list is intact and the hold count returns to zero.\n- Removing the result hold, the send hold, the start-up hold or the attach call makes it fail. The attach case needs a worker that reads its list without allocating, which the test now has.\n- 60 runs in a row passed on the Mac and 60 on the NUC (x86). band_pool_collect passed 40 runs on the NUC. The 18 Immix unit tests pass on both machines.\n- Python conformance 50 of 50, Lua conformance 46 of 46 and official_suite 4 of 4 pass with the flag on. No cancelled stop appears in the logs.\n\nKnown limits.\n- unhold searches from the back. A channel with very many queued messages pays a linear scan per receive. This is fine for the plugin's use today. If a program queues heavily, a multiset index fixes it.\n- A message that arrives while its receiver closes can stay held, which keeps one object alive. A thread that is never joined keeps its result.\n- A closure built in Rust with ZrtlClosure::new captures Rust values that no collection reads. Only closures from compiled code have a scanned environment.\n- A thread that attaches and then runs a long loop delays a collection. The loop has no allocation, wait or safepoint. The 20 ms timeout then cancels the stop. Compiled loops do not poll yet.\n- Threads started by the plugin are not announced to a host heap other than Immix.\n\nStep 5 is next: stress loops on both machines, a four-thread row set, the 43 rows, then the default.","files":null}]}